<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Podhale</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Podhale is a highland region of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> at the foot of the Tatra Mountains, centred on Nowy Targ and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zakopane">Zakopane</a>. Its Górale highlanders are known for their distinctive dialect, costume, music and wooden architecture. In 1918, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> collapsed, Zakopane and Podhale briefly governed themselves before joining the new Polish state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/podhale.jpg" alt="Podhale" width="320" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Blooo · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nt-223.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
